<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.13.3: Matching Tables, Equations, and Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00289</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.13.3: Matching Tables, Equations, and Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>0eb2a137-57a6-49eb-b93e-4925742f8916</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Take turns with your partner to match a graph with each set of <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/699661913d8187811b1278b918742716ff9c136e">matching cards</link>. Eventually all the cards will be sorted into groups of four cards (an equation, situation, table, and graph).</para>
      <para>For each match that you find, explain to your partner how you know it’s a match. Ask your partner if they agree with your thinking.</para>
      <para>For each match that your partner finds, listen carefully to their explanation. If you disagree, discuss your thinking and work to reach an agreement.</para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What did you and your partner agree are the cards that match the equation on card 1?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Cards 1, 11, 20, 32</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What did you and your partner agree are the cards that match the equation on card 2?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Cards 2, 16, 18, 25</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What did you and your partner agree are the cards that match the equation on card 3?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Cards 3, 10, 21, 29</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What did you and your partner agree are the cards that match the equation on card 4?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Cards 4, 9, 23, 30</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#5-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What did you and your partner agree are the cards that match the equation on card 5?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Cards 5, 12, 22, 31</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What did you and your partner agree are the cards that match the equation on card 6?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Cards 6, 15, 24, 26</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What did you and your partner agree are the cards that match the equation on card 7?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Cards 7, 14, 19, 27</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What did you and your partner agree are the cards that match the equation on card 8?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Cards 8, 13, 17, 28</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check">
      <para>Which of the following is a representation of \(y = 30x\)?</para>
    </note>
    <section>
      <title>Linear Equations and Their Representations</title>
      <para>Linear equations can be written from situations, tables of values, and graphs.</para>
      <para>Here is a linear situation:</para>
      <para>Sadie pays $15 a month for ballet classes plus $5 each lesson she takes.</para>
      <para>First, make a table of values to represent how much Sadie pays for the first 5 lessons.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
        <tgroup cols="7">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Number of Lessons</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>0</entry>
              <entry>1</entry>
              <entry>2</entry>
              <entry>3</entry>
              <entry>4</entry>
              <entry>5</entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Total Cost</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>15</entry>
              <entry>20</entry>
              <entry>25</entry>
              <entry>30</entry>
              <entry>35</entry>
              <entry>40</entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
      <para>Next, graph all of the points:</para>
      <para>
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_15_08_01.jpg" />
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para>Looking at the slope and \(y\)-intercepts of the table and graph, write an equation.</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
      <para>When \(x = 0\), \(y = 15\) so the \(y\)-intercept is 15.</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the slope.</para>
      <para>Slope is the change in \(y\) over the change in \(x\). For this table, the change in \(y\) is 43 and the change in \(x\) is 1. The slope is 3.</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
      <para>In the slope-intercept form, \(y=mx+b\), \(m\) is the slope and \(b\) is the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
      <para>The equation is \(y = 5x+15\).</para>
      <note class="try">
        <title>Try It: Linear Equations and Their Representations</title>
        <!--Q#-->
  
    <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Write an equation from the table:</para>
            <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
              <tgroup cols="6">
                <tbody>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>0</entry>
                    <entry>1</entry>
                    <entry>2</entry>
                    <entry>3</entry>
                    <entry>4</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>7</entry>
                    <entry>11</entry>
                    <entry>15</entry>
                    <entry>19</entry>
                    <entry>23</entry>
                  </row>
                </tbody>
              </tgroup>
            </table>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>Here is how to write an equation from a table.</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
            <para>When \(x = 0\), \(y = 7\) so the \(y\)-intercept is 7.</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the slope.</para>
            <para>Slope is the change in \(y\) over the change in \(x\). For this table, the change in \(y\) is 4 and the change in \(x\) is 1. The slope is 4.</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
            <para>In the slope-intercept form, \(y=mx+b\), \(m\) is the slope and \(b\) is the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
            <para>The equation is \(y = 4x +7\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
